# Corrections to the address a session signs in with, for commit authorship.
#
# Left to itself, Claude Code commits as `Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>`.
# That address belongs to the GitHub account `claude`, so every such commit
# is counted towards that account in this repository's contributor
# statistics rather than towards the person who asked for the change and
# owns it. `.claude/hooks/commit-identity.sh` records the signed-in person
# as the author instead, and leaves the agent as the committer.
#
# It uses the session's own address (CLAUDE_CODE_USER_EMAIL) unless there is
# a row for it here. A row is needed only when that address is not one the
# person's GitHub account carries: GitHub attributes a commit to whichever
# account holds the author address, so an address it cannot place counts for
# nobody. Either add the sign-in address to your GitHub account (Settings ->
# Emails) or map it here to one already on it — commonly the primary address
# or <id>+<login>@users.noreply.github.com.
#
# You do not need a row to be attributed correctly, and no row is a guess:
# a person the table does not know gets their own address, never someone
# else's. `.github/workflows/attribution.yml` fails a pull request whose
# commits carry an author GitHub cannot attribute, which is what turns a
# missing row into something you find out about on the PR rather than in the
# statistics months later.
#
# The committer stays the agent and every commit keeps its `Co-Authored-By:`
# trailer, so where the change came from is still on the record. What moves
# is only who GitHub counts it for.
#
# Format (one row per line, `#` starts a comment):
#   <sign-in address> = <git name> <<git email>>

